p\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Karate Champ\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.giantbomb.com/games/3030-7250/\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Karate Champ\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://giantbomb.com/karate-champ/3030-7250/\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Karate Champ\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.giantbomb.com/wd/3030-7250/\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ_-_Player_Vs_Player\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Dou\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Karate Champ | |\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://StrategyWiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Taisen_Karate_Dou\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Karate Champ\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.uvlist.net/game-106-Karate+Champ\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Main Restorations Software Audio/Jukebox/MP3 Everything Else Buy/Sell/Trade\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://forum.arcadecontrols.com/index.php/topic,12043.0.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"primetimeamusements.com\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://primetimeamusements.com/?p=22397\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Shack's Arcade Corner: Karate Champ\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.shacknews.com/article/109773/shacks-arcade-corner-karate-champ?amphtml=1\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"api.regvault.org\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/757c445d57800f0b80c12cbcb6b1ceab/manual\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Main Restorations Software Audio/Jukebox/MP3 Everything Else Buy/Sell/Trade\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://forum.arcadecontrols.com/index.php/topic,6383.0.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Main Restorations Software Audio/Jukebox/MP3 Everything Else Buy/Sell/Trade\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://forum.arcadecontrols.com/index.php/topic,24911.0.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Karate Champ\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://game.nite07.com/game/6770cb8dd6407ee0c1297c1a\\\"}]\\n\\nThe search confirmed the basic control scheme and several move names, but it did not turn up a clean move list, and I found nothing on a \\\"sweep\\\" or \\\"flip.\\\"\\n\\n- **Controls:** The left joystick handles movement and posture, and the right handles attacks. Using the two joysticks in combination yields 24 different controls, per one fan write-up. Another source puts the total at seventeen unique moves, so the counts disagree.\\n- **Move names:** Front kick, back kick, round kick, low kick, reverse punch, upper punch, block, and retreat all show up in the sources. One table lists a Back Round Kick as well.\\n- **Combinations:** StrategyWiki has a grid mapping left and right joystick directions to moves, but the table text came through scrambled, so I can't reliably read which combination produces which move. A second copy of the table on Primetime Amusements is easier to read, though the two layouts don't fully match. For exact mappings, check the StrategyWiki page directly or the MAME input setup.\\n- **Sweep and flip:** Neither term appeared in any result, so I can't confirm either move exists under those names.\\n\\n\\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"80ce591a-c569-4b0f-8054-2607ac336fcc\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T00:55:05.363Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"query\":\"Karate Champ 1984 arcade move list dual joystick combinations front kick back kick sweep flip\",\"results\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"srvtoolu_0179svwuuqB6bddY3joE7NRj\",\"content\":[{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Topics/19207\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453704\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453634\"},{\"title\":\"Submission #5529: £e_Nécroyeur's Arcade Karate Champ in 09:07.65\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/5529S\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://giantbomb.com/wiki/Games/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://www.giantbomb.com/games/3030-7250/\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://giantbomb.com/karate-champ/3030-7250/\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://www.giantbomb.com/wd/3030-7250/\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ_-_Player_Vs_Player\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Dou\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ | |\",\"url\":\"https://StrategyWiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Taisen_Karate_Dou\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://www.uvlist.net/game-106-Karate+Champ\"},{\"title\":\"Main Restorations Software Audio/Jukebox/MP3 Everything Else Buy/Sell/Trade\",\"url\":\"https://forum.arcadecontrols.com/index.php/topic,12043.0.html\"},{\"title\":\"primetimeamusements.com\",\"url\":\"https://primetimeamusements.com/?p=22397\"},{\"title\":\"Shack's Arcade Corner: Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://www.shacknews.com/article/109773/shacks-arcade-corner-karate-champ?amphtml=1\"},{\"title\":\"api.regvault.org\",\"url\":\"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/757c445d57800f0b80c12cbcb6b1ceab/manual\"},{\"title\":\"Main Restorations Software Audio/Jukebox/MP3 Everything Else Buy/Sell/Trade\",\"url\":\"https://forum.arcadecontrols.com/index.php/topic,6383.0.html\"},{\"title\":\"Main Restorations Software Audio/Jukebox/MP3 Everything Else Buy/Sell/Trade\",\"url\":\"https://forum.arcadecontrols.com/index.php/topic,24911.0.html\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://game.nite07.com/game/6770cb8dd6407ee0c1297c1a\"}]},\"The search confirmed the basic control scheme and several move names, but it did not turn up a clean move list, and I found nothing on a \\\"sweep\\\" or \\\"flip.\\\"\\n\\n- **Controls:** The left joystick handles movement and posture, and the right handles attacks. Using the two joysticks in combination yields 24 different controls, per one fan write-up. Another source puts the total at seventeen unique moves, so the counts disagree.\\n- **Move names:** Front kick, back kick, round kick, low kick, reverse punch, upper punch, block, and retreat all show up in the sources. One table lists a Back Round Kick as well.\\n- **Combinations:** StrategyWiki has a grid mapping left and right joystick directions to moves, but the table text came through scrambled, so I can't reliably read which combination produces which move. A second copy of the table on Primetime Amusements is easier to read, though the two layouts don't fully match. For exact mappings, check the StrategyWiki page directly or the MAME input setup.\\n- **Sweep and flip:** Neither term appeared in any result, so I can't confirm either move exists under those names.\"],\"durationSeconds\":7.1058380149999865,\"searchCount\":1},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_018V9XufMZ37nPhKWgHstgFt\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}","+{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01A6J4EfyvcxAfUpVkfdFWLk\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"## Karate Champ: Gameplay Facts from the Article\\n\\n**Developer, publisher, release**\\n- Developed and published by Data East. Berkeley Softworks converted the Apple II and Commodore 64 versions.\\n- Arcade: Japan in May 1984; worldwide in September 1984.\\n- Apple II/C64: North America, October 1985.\\n- NES: North America, December 1986.\\n- Famicom Disk System: Japan, July 22, 1988.\\n- The article does not name a designer.\\n\\n**Versions**\\n- The original single-player version was Japan-exclusive. The two-player \\\"Player vs Player\\\" edition, released internationally as *Karate Champ*, became the basis for the home versions.\\n- Player vs Player uses the same hardware and mechanics, with improved speech, new sprites, and a new title screen.\\n\\n**Controls and moves**\\n- Two joysticks control the game, and players can execute 24 moves. The article does not explain how moves are input or list them.\\n\\n**Scoring and match rules**\\n- There is no health bar. A landed hit ends the round.\\n- The hitter earns a full point or a half point. Top-ten entries also get a numeric score, which doesn't affect the match result.\\n- The first to two points wins. Matches are best-of-three.\\n- Losing a battle ends the game.\\n- The judge's digitized speech calls out \\\"Half point\\\" or \\\"Full point.\\\" The speech is in Japanese in the original version.\\n- The article does not mention a timer, belts or ranks, or specific judge decisions.\\n\\n**Structure and stages**\\n- Player one controls the white-gi karateka. The red-gi opponent is controlled by player two or the CPU.\\n- Bonus rounds follow for the successful player, and the pattern repeats with harder rounds.\\n- The first stage is a dojo. Later stages take place at the same tournament stadium.\\n\\n**Player vs Player mode**\\n- Two players compete over a girl in twelve locations, with a best-of-three match for each.\\n- After each match, bonus rounds include evading potted plants, kicking through boards, and stopping a charging bull.\\n- Locations include a harbor, a garden with a view of Mount Fuji, a bamboo forest, a tree trunk over a chasm, a Japanese ship's stern, a road or runway, Holland with windmills, a big city, a North American desert with tipis, a Japanese garden, a dojo, and a night forest path.\\n- Completing all twelve scenes starts a second loop, and completing that loop completes the game.\\n- The Japanese title is *Taisen Karate Dō: Bishōjo Seishun Hen*.\\n\\n**Not covered in the article:** CPU opponent details beyond the red-gi role, attract mode, and the judge's character beyond his speech.\\n\\n**Ports and re-releases**\\n- NES: inspired by the Player vs Player edition. The My Arcade version removed the referee's voice and replaced the music. Two-player mode was removed there too.\\n- PlayStation 2: Hamster emulation of the original arcade version only.\\n- Arcade Archives (PS4 2015, Switch 2019) and Console Archives (Switch 2 and PS5, September 2026, per the article).\\n- iOS (2010, Revolutionary Concepts): a remake with a difficulty mode and Bluetooth or Wi-Fi play. The iPad version is *Karate Champ XL*.\\n- My Arcade (2018), Evercade, and Polymega (late 2024) releases.\\n\\n**Sales and reception**\\n- Sold 10,000 arcade cabinets within two months and became Data East's best-selling arcade game.\\n- Shipped 30,000 US arcade units by April 1985.\\n- Was the highest-grossing arcade game of 1985.\\n- On home computers, earned a Diamond Award from the Software Publishers Association for over 500,000 units (January 1989).\\n\\n**Legacy and influence**\\n- Credited with establishing the one-on-one fighting genre. The article notes it set conventions such as best-of-three matches, special-move inputs, and bonus rounds.\\n- The Player vs Player edition was described as the first fighting game to let two players fight each other.\\n- Influenced *Yie Ar Kung-Fu* and *The Way of the Exploding Fist*. John Tobias cited it as the primary gameplay inspiration for *Mortal Kombat*.\\n- Data East sued counterfeiters and sued Epyx over *World Karate Championship*. The court found the games were not substantially similar.\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"0252d7cd-b6b9-4ba1-9ec4-75e60346b883\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T00:55:11.656Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"bytes\":235108,\"code\":200,\"codeText\":\"OK\",\"result\":\"## Karate Champ: Gameplay Facts from the Article\\n\\n**Developer, publisher, release**\\n- Developed and published by Data East. Berkeley Softworks converted the Apple II and Commodore 64 versions.\\n- Arcade: Japan in May 1984; worldwide in September 1984.\\n- Apple II/C64: North America, October 1985.\\n- NES: North America, December 1986.\\n- Famicom Disk System: Japan, July 22, 1988.\\n- The article does not name a designer.\\n\\n**Versions**\\n- The original single-player version was Japan-exclusive. The two-player \\\"Player vs Player\\\" edition, released internationally as *Karate Champ*, became the basis for the home versions.\\n- Player vs Player uses the same hardware and mechanics, with improved speech, new sprites, and a new title screen.\\n\\n**Controls and moves**\\n- Two joysticks control the game, and players can execute 24 moves. The article does not explain how moves are input or list them.\\n\\n**Scoring and match rules**\\n- There is no health bar. A landed hit ends the round.\\n- The hitter earns a full point or a half point. Top-ten entries also get a numeric score, which doesn't affect the match result.\\n- The first to two points wins. Matches are best-of-three.\\n- Losing a battle ends the game.\\n- The judge's digitized speech calls out \\\"Half point\\\" or \\\"Full point.\\\" The speech is in Japanese in the original version.\\n- The article does not mention a timer, belts or ranks, or specific judge decisions.\\n\\n**Structure and stages**\\n- Player one controls the white-gi karateka. The red-gi opponent is controlled by player two or the CPU.\\n- Bonus rounds follow for the successful player, and the pattern repeats with harder rounds.\\n- The first stage is a dojo. Later stages take place at the same tournament stadium.\\n\\n**Player vs Player mode**\\n- Two players compete over a girl in twelve locations, with a best-of-three match for each.\\n- After each match, bonus rounds include evading potted plants, kicking through boards, and stopping a charging bull.\\n- Locations include a harbor, a garden with a view of Mount Fuji, a bamboo forest, a tree trunk over a chasm, a Japanese ship's stern, a road or runway, Holland with windmills, a big city, a North American desert with tipis, a Japanese garden, a dojo, and a night forest path.\\n- Completing all twelve scenes starts a second loop, and completing that loop completes the game.\\n- The Japanese title is *Taisen Karate Dō: Bishōjo Seishun Hen*.\\n\\n**Not covered in the article:** CPU opponent details beyond the red-gi role, attract mode, and the judge's character beyond his speech.\\n\\n**Ports and re-releases**\\n- NES: inspired by the Player vs Player edition. The My Arcade version removed the referee's voice and replaced the music. Two-player mode was removed there too.\\n- PlayStation 2: Hamster emulation of the original arcade version only.\\n- Arcade Archives (PS4 2015, Switch 2019) and Console Archives (Switch 2 and PS5, September 2026, per the article).\\n- iOS (2010, Revolutionary Concepts): a remake with a difficulty mode and Bluetooth or Wi-Fi play. The iPad version is *Karate Champ XL*.\\n- My Arcade (2018), Evercade, and Polymega (late 2024) releases.\\n\\n**Sales and reception**\\n- Sold 10,000 arcade cabinets within two months and became Data East's best-selling arcade game.\\n- Shipped 30,000 US arcade units by April 1985.\\n- Was the highest-grossing arcade game of 1985.\\n- On home computers, earned a Diamond Award from the Software Publishers Association for over 500,000 units (January 1989).\\n\\n**Legacy and influence**\\n- Credited with establishing the one-on-one fighting genre. The article notes it set conventions such as best-of-three matches, special-move inputs, and bonus rounds.\\n- The Player vs Player edition was described as the first fighting game to let two players fight each other.\\n- Influenced *Yie Ar Kung-Fu* and *The Way of the Exploding Fist*. John Tobias cited it as the primary gameplay inspiration for *Mortal Kombat*.\\n- Data East sued counterfeiters and sued Epyx over *World Karate Championship*. The court found the games were not substantially similar.\",\"durationMs\":14049,\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ\"},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01A6J4EfyvcxAfUpVkfdFWLk\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174804__x8cc180/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":304,"oldLines":3,"newStart":304,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01JGMmrVxEdjq6nwrC9CGULd\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"2505:    sh.show(name || 'settings'); return true;\\n3879:  show(mode = 'load', done) {\\n9168:   * - screens: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\\n9220:  show(name, spec = {}) {\\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174804__x8cc180/work\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"01565e32-5dd5-4107-91e6-1b53667edea6\",\"uuid\":\"350675fa-155b-43eb-9fc2-fd1052f9288e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T00:55:04.900Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"2505:    sh.show(name || 'settings'); return true;\\n3879:  show(mode = 'load', done) {\\n9168:   * - screens: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\\n9220:  show(name, spec = {}) {\",\"stderr\":\"\\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174804__x8cc180/work\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01JGMmrVxEdjq6nwrC9CGULd\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opu