{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3VSbdtoGuHMBtSKuak","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01T1R6vyS15yXpYn36dbmkqG","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174737__x193f88/work/PLAN.md","limit":120},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1082,"cache_read_input_tokens":508502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1082},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40","uuid":"9762dde2-4d3e-40e8-a2a3-b8da9dad711c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:22.269Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3VSMVv886eaLtySHxp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01T1R6vyS15yXpYn36dbmkqG":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174737__x193f88/work/PLAN.md","limit":120}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T1R6vyS15yXpYn36dbmkqG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Eye of the Bolt-Holder\n2\t\n3\tA build plan for a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by **Eye of the Beholder** (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991). It is built on the my-3D2dge engine and ships as one HTML file. It is written for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from TASK.md and this file alone.\n4\t\n5\t- **Title.** I brainstormed 50 playful riffs on the source's name and picked one with a real RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned index 5): **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**.\n6\t- **File-name forms.** GameTitle = `EyeOfTheBoltHolder`, SOURCE = `EyeOfTheBeholder1991`. Fill these into your TASK.md's bracket pattern, for example `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<timestamp>`.\n7\t- **Pitch.** Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks, is being locked in from below. Doors bolt themselves, wells cap shut, and a granary will not open for axes. The Council of Keys sends a party of four (later up to six) down through the Drains, the Tumbler Halls of the dead locksmiths' guild, the Gloam Deeps of the eye-cult and, at last, the Eye's Vault. The party moves one cell at a time in real time: it swings each hand as it comes off cooldown, memorizes spells at camp, eats rations, weighs plates with rocks and finds the loose stone. At the bottom it turns three Great Locks to strip the cage from **the Bolt-Holder**, a floating amber Eye held in a cage of seven iron bolts that hurls lightning, gazes joints stiff and rains iron.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. Ground rules for the builders\n12\t\n13\t1. **TASK.md wins on deliverables.** Names, folders, the single HTML, the About screen, the reply and `exit-interview.json` follow your TASK.md exactly. This plan says how; §11 has the templates.\n14\t2. **Folders.** Your dev folder is the bracket name; the bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` go in the root beside TASK.md.\n15\t   - Keep every clone, cache and `node_modules` inside your dev folder (in `_ref/` and similar). `/tmp` on this machine is a shared tmpfs and it was full during planning (a `git clone` into it failed with \"No space left on device\").\n16\t   - Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need. Other agents share the machine.\n17\t3. **Processes and ports.**\n18\t   - Prefer `file://` and headless Node.\n19\t   - When you need a server (only the AI Hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and read the port it gets. Check that the page is your game before trusting a result.\n20\t   - Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill, killall or `fuser -k`.\n21\t4. **Tests.** One command runs every test and prints only failures plus a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n22\t5. **No stubs.** Everything in this plan is required unless it is marked *optional*. If time runs short, cut quantity (fewer side items, smaller side rooms), never a system or a floor. There must be no TODOs, placeholders or dead menu entries.\n23\t6. **Engine changes are allowed.** Keep each one small and list it in `PATCHES.md` (what and why). The About screen and the Engine Feedback answers draw on it.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## 1. Research summary\n28\t\n29\t### 1.1 The source, and what we keep\n30\t\n31\t**Confirmed facts** (Giant Bomb, MobyGames, oldgames.sk):\n32\t- Eye of the Beholder was developed by Westwood Associates and published by SSI in 1991, for MS-DOS and almost at once for the Amiga.\n33\t- A party of four created heroes is joined by up to two recruitable NPCs.\n34\t- The Lords of Waterdeep send the party into the sewers, and the way out is sealed behind them.\n35\t- At the bottom waits the beholder crime lord Xanathar.\n36\t\n37\t**What made it distinctive** (from knowledge of the game, not re-verified), and how this plan uses each part:\n38\t\n39\t| Source trait | How we turn it into our game |\n40\t|---|---|\n41\t| First person on a grid, 90° turns, everything in real time | Step, turn and strafe one cell at a time. Monsters act on their own clocks, so there are no turns. |\n42\t| Party of 4 to 6 in rows; the front two melee; the back rows need reach, missiles or spells | Formation of three rows of two, with reach weapons in row 2 and missiles, thrown weapons and spells from anywhere (§4.6) |\n43\t| Every hand is an action; it greys out on cooldown; damage shows on the portraits | Two hand slots on every party card, each with a cooldown wipe and damage pops (§5.2) |\n44\t| Vancian magic: mages learn from scrolls into a spellbook and memorize; clerics pray; both at camp | Camp menu with Rest, Memorize, Pray and Scribe (§4.8, §4.9) |\n45\t| Paperdoll and backpack; the item held on the cursor is thrown, placed and dropped; food and rations | The party's **Hand** (the held item), throwing, putting down and alcoves; a food meter (§4.5, §4.9) |\n46\t| Puzzle vocabulary: pressure plates held down by items, levers, buttons, keys of many shapes, illusory walls, loose-stone switches, teleporters, spinners, pits to the level below, wall niches, inscriptions, dart traps, hidden \"special quests\" | All of them, wired as data (§4.2). Each floor has one hidden deed (special quest). |\n47\t| Monster groups share a cell; the sidestep \"dance\" | Cell capacity by size. Attack wind-ups that a step can dodge make sidestepping an intended skill (§4.7). |\n48\t| Terse second-person messages, a stately intro, formal NPCs, a few moral choices | Style guide and scripts (§7.2, §7.3), plus Veil's choice on floor 3 |\n49\t\n50\t**What we change:**\n51\t- The world and characters are original: no Waterdeep, Forgotten Realms, Xanathar, beholders or AD&D rule text.\n52\t- The UI is controller-first for a TV at 10 feet.\n53\t- Steps are tweened, and the 3D view fills the screen with dynamic light and shadow.\n54\t- Monster attacks are telegraphed.\n55\t- Music has combat layers, and voices are babble.\n56\t- Hints escalate, so no puzzle can strand the player.\n57\t\n58\t### 1.2 The engine (my-3D2dge v0.85.2)\n59\t\n60\t- **The handoff** (`dist/handoff/`) is the 2D engine. `my-3d2dge.js` is readable and includes every module, among them `grid` (E.Grid, E.Fog), `story` (E.Story) and `rpg`. It also holds the docs (API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md), the tools (`tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `replay.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`), 22 2D kits and a template game whose `game/test.mjs` shows how to test without a browser.\n61\t- **The 3D engine.** The handoff's genre playbook sends \"First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder)\" to the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera and the **3D CRAWLER** kit. That engine is not in the handoff folder but sits in the same repository: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, v0.85.2, an ES module whose header is its manual. It uses three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER` to the 2D engine, which still runs the loop, the scenes, the input, the menus, the sound and the rigs.\n62\t- **The crawler slice** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`, 561 lines) already does most of the core loop:\n63\t  - cells of 2×2 tiles with tweened steps, turns, strafes and a bump lurch;\n64\t  - a fixed camera at eye height 25;\n65\t  - a party of four on cards with `rig.drawPortrait`;\n66\t  - gates (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock), a lever `THING`, plates and a dart trap;\n67\t  - monsters walked on `E.Grid` paths, an `E.Fog` automap and a bolt projectile;\n68\t  - real time via `E.Turns 'atb'`, and a bot.\n69\t\n70\t  Use it as the reference for the camera, the tweens and the things. Restructure the rest: the kit mixes rules with the 3D world, and we separate them (§3.5).\n71\t- **Spike run during planning.** I took the repository's `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-crawler.html` and pointed its import map at jsDelivr: `three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`. These are byte-identical to the vendored copies by SHA-256. Opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 (Playwright 1.63.0) with SwiftShader WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl#crawler`):\n72\t  - it booted with no console errors;\n73\t  - `CRAWLER.run(600)` gave the same hash twice (`e3880e33`);\n74\t  - `CRAWLER.bot()` won: lever, key, unlock, plate, Warden and stair, all four alive.\n75\t\n76\t  The native look is chunky pixel 3D with brick walls, flagstone floors and live portrait cards. **So the core technical bet works: one HTML file, opened from disk, with the engines inlined and three.js and Rapier from a CDN.**\n77\t- **Engine names this plan relies on** (look up details in API-INDEX.md and the 3D header):\n78\t\n79\t  | Area | Names |\n80\t  |---|---|\n81\t  | Game and scenes | `new E.Game({ canvas, res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input, menu })`, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { steps } })`, `game.go`, `game.advance(s)`, `game.debug` |\n82\t  | Timing and scripts | `game.timeScale`, `game.script` (coroutines), `game.tween`, `game.after` |\n83\t  | Input | `game.input` (`pressed`, `down`, `tap`, `hold`, `press(code)`, `clicked()`, `pointer()`, `padAxes`, `lastDevice`, `label(action)`, `glyphs`) |\n84\t  | Settings and saves | `game.settings.define`, `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave), `menu: { items, settings, saves }` |\n85\t  | Sound | `game.audio` (`define`, `sfx`, `music`, `layer`, `sting`, `duck`, `ctx`) |\n86\t  | Text, UI and rigs | `E.Dialog` (`talk.shown`, `talk.voice`), `E.Story`, `E.Menu`, `E.ui.box/bar`, `E.font.text/title`, `E.Humanoid` / `E.Blob` with `rig.drawPortrait`, `E.MOVES` |\n87\t  | Grid module | `E.Grid` (`path`), `E.Fog` |\n88\t  | 3D world | `new E.World3D(game, { level, … })`, `world.thing(kind, …)`, `E.World3D.THINGS.<kind>` (pen-drawn things), `world.mob`, `world.pen`, `world.glow`, `world.light`, `world.arc`, `world.project`, `world.camera` (`'fixed'`), `overlay: { fov }` plus `world.overlay.pen` (first-person hands), `world.prepare()`, `world.dispose()` |\n89\t  | 3D settings | `E.World3D.SETTINGS` (cards or puppets, picture resolution, shadows, filter) |\n90\t\n91\t### 1.3 AI Hub (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n92\t\n93\t- **Modes.** `parallel`, `ghost` (each copy runs its own round and shows the others' characters when they are in the same area) and `shared-world`.\n94\t- **The client.** `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. In a session it seeds `Math.random`, gives the page its own in-memory storage and hands fullscreen to the hub.\n95\t- **Tags.** `hub:session`, `hub:session-players`, `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal` (at most 60 characters), `hub:session-howto` (at most 80) and `hub:session-minutes`.\n96\t- **The flow.** `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` returns `info`, or `null` when there is no hub (then the game plays on its own). `AIHub.ready()` is called once, with no key press. The round starts on `start`, and play begins when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n97\t- **Reporting.**\n98\t  - `progress({ score, label })` is taken about once a second.\n99\t  - `result({ time | score })` is sent once.\n100\t  - On `time-up` the game reports within `graceMs`.\n101\t  - On `end` input and sound stop.\n102\t- **Messages.** `send(data, { reliable })` takes JSON of at most 16 KB, at most 20 a second.\n103\t- **Rules.**\n104\t  - The network goes only through the hub.\n105\t  - There are no dialogs or popups, and no quitting or returning to the title in a session.\n106\t  - Names come from the hub and never become HTML.\n107\t  - Options is ignored while Create is held.\n108\t- **The test.** The harness (`harness.html` plus `bridge.js` and `round.js`, served beside the game) passes the game when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. Run it with `?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`, then with `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1` and `&scenario=host-leave`.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 2. The game at a glance\n113\t\n114\t- **Genre.** A real-time, first-person, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n115\t- **Perspective.**\n116\t  - First person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns.\n117\t  - A top-down parchment automap screen.\n118\t  - Full-screen overlay screens (party, camp, map), drawn over the dimmed 3D view.\n119\t- **Pillars.**\n120\t  1. *Every cell is a decision.* Step, turn and sidestep in real time."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40","uuid":"9ec11eb1-2331-41c1-934c-8168856842e6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:22.288Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174737__x193f88/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Eye of the Bolt-Holder\n\nA build plan for a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by **Eye of the Beholder** (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991). It is built on the my-3D2dge engine and ships as one HTML file. It is written for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from TASK.md and this file alone.\n\n- **Title.** I brainstormed 50 playful riffs on the source's name and picked one with a real RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned index 5): **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**.\n- **File-name forms.** GameTitle = `EyeOfTheBoltHolder`, SOURCE = `EyeOfTheBeholder1991`. Fill these into your TASK.md's bracket pattern, for example `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<timestamp>`.\n- **Pitch.** Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks, is being locked in from below. Doors bolt themselves, wells cap shut, and a granary will not open for axes. The Council of Keys sends a party of four (later up to six) down through the Drains, the Tumbler Halls of the dead locksmiths' guild, the Gloam Deeps of the eye-cult and, at last, the Eye's Vault. The party moves one cell at a time in real time: it swings each hand as it comes off cooldown, memorizes spells at camp, eats rations, weighs plates with rocks and finds the loose stone. At the bottom it turns three Great Locks to strip the cage from **the Bolt-Holder**, a floating amber Eye held in a cage of seven iron bolts that hurls lightning, gazes joints stiff and rains iron.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules for the builders\n\n1. **TASK.md wins on deliverables.** Names, folders, the single HTML, the About screen, the reply and `exit-interview.json` follow your TASK.md exactly. This plan says how; §11 has the templates.\n2. **Folders.** Your dev folder is the bracket name; the bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` go in the root beside TASK.md.\n   - Keep every clone, cache and `node_modules` inside your dev folder (in `_ref/` and similar). `/tmp` on this machine is a shared tmpfs and it was full during planning (a `git clone` into it failed with \"No space left on device\").\n   - Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need. Other agents share the machine.\n3. **Processes and ports.**\n   - Prefer `file://` and headless Node.\n   - When you need a server (only the AI Hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and read the port it gets. Check that the page is your game before trusting a result.\n   - Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill, killall or `fuser -k`.\n4. **Tests.** One command runs every test and prints only failures plus a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n5. **No stubs.** Everything in this plan is required unless it is marked *optional*. If time runs short, cut quantity (fewer side items, smaller side rooms), never a system or a floor. There must be no TODOs, placeholders or dead menu entries.\n6. **Engine changes are allowed.** Keep each one small and list it in `PATCHES.md` (what and why). The About screen and the Engine Feedback answers draw on it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research summary\n\n### 1.1 The source, and what we keep\n\n**Confirmed facts** (Giant Bomb, MobyGames, oldgames.sk):\n- Eye of the Beholder was developed by Westwood Associates and published by SSI in 1991, for MS-DOS and almost at once for the Amiga.\n- A party of four created heroes is joined by up to two recruitable NPCs.\n- The Lords of Waterdeep send the party into the sewers, and the way out is sealed behind them.\n- At the bottom waits the beholder crime lord Xanathar.\n\n**What made it distinctive** (from knowledge of the game, not re-verified), and how this plan uses each part:\n\n| Source trait | How we turn it into our game |\n|---|---|\n| First person on a grid, 90° turns, everything in real time | Step, turn and strafe one cell at a time. Monsters act on their own clocks, so there are no turns. |\n| Party of 4 to 6 in rows; the front two melee; the back rows need reach, missiles or spells | Formation of three rows of two, with reach weapons in row 2 and missiles, thrown weapons and spells from anywhere (§4.6) |\n| Every hand is an action; it greys out on cooldown; damage shows on the portraits | Two hand slots on every party card, each with a cooldown wipe and damage pops (§5.2) |\n| Vancian magic: mages learn from scrolls into a spellbook and memorize; clerics pray; both at camp | Camp menu with Rest, Memorize, Pray and Scribe (§4.8, §4.9) |\n| Paperdoll and backpack; the item held on the cursor is thrown, placed and dropped; food and rations | The party's **Hand** (the held item), throwing, putting down and alcoves; a food meter (§4.5, §4.9) |\n| Puzzle vocabulary: pressure plates held down by items, levers, buttons, keys of many shapes, illusory walls, loose-stone switches, teleporters, spinners, pits to the level below, wall niches, inscriptions, dart traps, hidden \"special quests\" | All of them, wired as data (§4.2). Each floor has one hidden deed (special quest). |\n| Monster groups share a cell; the sidestep \"dance\" | Cell capacity by size. Attack wind-ups that a step can dodge make sidestepping an intended skill (§4.7). |\n| Terse second-person messages, a stately intro, formal NPCs, a few moral choices | Style guide and scripts (§7.2, §7.3), plus Veil's choice on floor 3 |\n\n**What we change:**\n- The world and characters are original: no Waterdeep, Forgotten Realms, Xanathar, beholders or AD&D rule text.\n- The UI is controller-first for a TV at 10 feet.\n- Steps are tweened, and the 3D view fills the screen with dynamic light and shadow.\n- Monster attacks are telegraphed.\n- Music has combat layers, and voices are babble.\n- Hints escalate, so no puzzle can strand the player.\n\n### 1.2 The engine (my-3D2dge v0.85.2)\n\n- **The handoff** (`dist/handoff/`) is the 2D engine. `my-3d2dge.js` is readable and includes every module, among them `grid` (E.Grid, E.Fog), `story` (E.Story) and `rpg`. It also holds the docs (API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md), the tools (`tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `replay.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`), 22 2D kits and a template game whose `game/test.mjs` shows how to test without a browser.\n- **The 3D engine.** The handoff's genre playbook sends \"First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder)\" to the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera and the **3D CRAWLER** kit. That engine is not in the handoff folder but sits in the same repository: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, v0.85.2, an ES module whose header is its manual. It uses three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER` to the 2D engine, which still runs the loop, the scenes, the input, the menus, the sound and the rigs.\n- **The crawler slice** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`, 561 lines) already does most of the core loop:\n  - cells of 2×2 tiles with tweened steps, turns, strafes and a bump lurch;\n  - a fixed camera at eye height 25;\n  - a party of four on cards with `rig.drawPortrait`;\n  - gates (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock), a lever `THING`, plates and a dart trap;\n  - monsters walked on `E.Grid` paths, an `E.Fog` automap and a bolt projectile;\n  - real time via `E.Turns 'atb'`, and a bot.\n\n  Use it as the reference for the camera, the tweens and the things. Restructure the rest: the kit mixes rules with the 3D world, and we separate them (§3.5).\n- **Spike run during planning.** I took the repository's `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-crawler.html` and pointed its import map at jsDelivr: `three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`. These are byte-identical to the vendored copies by SHA-256. Opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 (Playwright 1.63.0) with SwiftShader WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl#crawler`):\n  - it booted with no console errors;\n  - `CRAWLER.run(600)` gave the same hash twice (`e3880e33`);\n  - `CRAWLER.bot()` won: lever, key, unlock, plate, Warden and stair, all four alive.\n\n  The native look is chunky pixel 3D with brick walls, flagstone floors and live portrait cards. **So the core technical bet works: one HTML file, opened from disk, with the engines inlined and three.js and Rapier from a CDN.**\n- **Engine names this plan relies on** (look up details in API-INDEX.md and the 3D header):\n\n  | Area | Names |\n  |---|---|\n  | Game and scenes | `new E.Game({ canvas, res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input, menu })`, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { steps } })`, `game.go`, `game.advance(s)`, `game.debug` |\n  | Timing and scripts | `game.timeScale`, `game.script` (coroutines), `game.tween`, `game.after` |\n  | Input | `game.input` (`pressed`, `down`, `tap`, `hold`, `press(code)`, `clicked()`, `pointer()`, `padAxes`, `lastDevice`, `label(action)`, `glyphs`) |\n  | Settings and saves | `game.settings.define`, `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave), `menu: { items, settings, saves }` |\n  | Sound | `game.audio` (`define`, `sfx`, `music`, `layer`, `sting`, `duck`, `ctx`) |\n  | Text, UI and rigs | `E.Dialog` (`talk.shown`, `talk.voice`), `E.Story`, `E.Menu`, `E.ui.box/bar`, `E.font.text/title`, `E.Humanoid` / `E.Blob` with `rig.drawPortrait`, `E.MOVES` |\n  | Grid module | `E.Grid` (`path`), `E.Fog` |\n  | 3D world | `new E.World3D(game, { level, … })`, `world.thing(kind, …)`, `E.World3D.THINGS.<kind>` (pen-drawn things), `world.mob`, `world.pen`, `world.glow`, `world.light`, `world.arc`, `world.project`, `world.camera` (`'fixed'`), `overlay: { fov }` plus `world.overlay.pen` (first-person hands), `world.prepare()`, `world.dispose()` |\n  | 3D settings | `E.World3D.SETTINGS` (cards or puppets, picture resolution, shadows, filter) |\n\n### 1.3 AI Hub (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n\n- **Modes.** `parallel`, `ghost` (each copy runs its own round and shows the others' characters when they are in the same area) and `shared-world`.\n- **The client.** `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. In a session it seeds `Math.random`, gives the page its own in-memory storage and hands fullscreen to the hub.\n- **Tags.** `hub:session`, `hub:session-players`, `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal` (at most 60 characters), `hub:session-howto` (at most 80) and `hub:session-minutes`.\n- **The flow.** `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` returns `info`, or `null` when there is no hub (then the game plays on its own). `AIHub.ready()` is called once, with no key press. The round starts on `start`, and play begins when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n- **Reporting.**\n  - `progress({ score, label })` is taken about once a second.\n  - `result({ time | score })` is sent once.\n  - On `time-up` the game reports within `graceMs`.\n  - On `end` input and sound stop.\n- **Messages.** `send(data, { reliable })` takes JSON of at most 16 KB, at most 20 a second.\n- **Rules.**\n  - The network goes only through the hub.\n  - There are no dialogs or popups, and no quitting or returning to the title in a session.\n  - Names come from the hub and never become HTML.\n  - Options is ignored while Create is held.\n- **The test.** The harness (`harness.html` plus `bridge.js` and `round.js`, served beside the game) passes the game when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. Run it with `?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`, then with `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1` and `&scenario=host-leave`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game at a glance\n\n- **Genre.** A real-time, first-person, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n- **Perspective.**\n  - First person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns.\n  - A top-down parchment automap screen.\n  - Full-screen overlay screens (party, camp, map), drawn over the dimmed 3D view.\n- **Pillars.**\n  1. *Every cell is a decision.* Step, turn and sidestep in real time.","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2131}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01T1R6vyS15yXpYn36dbmkqG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
